{
  "format": "kingmaker",
  "version": 1,
  "kind": "scene",
  "id": "wharf-night",
  "title": "The wharves after dark",
  "description": "Downtime on the river stairs at night, between the Drowned Rat and the Heron.\n\n## Setting\n\nThe river stairs and the wharves after dark, before the midnight bells: the river high and black, fog coming off it, barge lanterns, night porters going down to the shifts, eel-sellers packing up, the bunting above gone grey in the dark, and downriver the painted heron of the last warehouse, its hoist working.\n\n## Premise\n\nDowntime: where does the player go next, and what do they make of Caerwyn on the way? (The lead: The Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves, is loading a barge tonight that sails on the ebb at the second bell after midnight; if Hobb talked, the gentleman comes at midnight to see it off.)\n\n## Hook\n\nA breath between one thing and the next.\n\n## Opening\n\nA little time has passed since the last scene ended (the hand-off says how): the player has left that place and is out in the town now (outside on the river stairs, the Drowned Rat behind them). In a line or two, say where the others from the last scene went: Sergeant Kell stays with the investigator (the King lent her to the case) unless what happened rules it out; anyone else goes their own way. Then show the town as it is now: the docks at night (the river high and black, fog, barge lanterns, the night shift going down, the Heron's hoist working downriver). From Caerwyn now, show what's new since the last scene: one or two things, by sight or by talk, each said once by someone it matters to. Two or three vivid details in all, no more. End with the player on their own feet, and ask what they do.\n\n## Cast\n\n### Sergeant Agnes Kell (kell)\n\n**Look:** Forties, a rain cape over her watch uniform (or a borrowed coat that fools nobody, after dark), a battered oilcloth notebook and a pencil stub she sharpens with a clasp knife; a truncheon she has never been seen to draw. Stands very still and watches people's hands, even in a crowd.\n\n**Voice:** Dry, calm and sparing: short sentences, and 'Noted.' only when something goes in the notebook. Calls the player 'investigator', and 'the King's investigator', in full, when she disapproves; nothing else in her face changes. Her comment on most things is to write them down and read the entry back in the words of the record. 'That's a theory, investigator. I can't arrest a theory.' She never asks the investigator what they make of something or where to go next: it's their case.\n\n**Wants:** Her own errand, which depends on the hour. In the afternoon: to go down the river stairs at dusk after the big man with the bandaged hand she followed there at noon, with the investigator or without, though she'd rather not walk into the Drowned Rat alone (Nan Quill put her out through its door twenty years ago). After dark on the wharves: to see the Heron's night loading with her own eyes, since a sergeant who sees the King's grain taken may arrest whoever takes it, and to keep the investigator out of the river while she does.\n\n**Fears:** The trail going cold while the city is busy with bunting. A witness in the river. A true record going in a drawer until after the Assembly.\n\n**Attitude:** As things stand between her and the investigator (her memories say how); practical and dry. She says where she's going and when, once, and goes, with the investigator or without; she doesn't steer them, wait on them, or tell them what to do. She answers what she's asked, reads back what she's written, and gives her own dry view of the town when it's wanted. If a child of the granary is sitting in a cell for Prout, she says so, and means to get them out. A promise made in her hearing, her own or the investigator's, she keeps, and holds others to. A name someone was promised would be kept out of it (a child who told what he heard, say) stays out of her notebook and out of anything she says or swears to from then on: she knows who it was, and writes and says 'a witness' instead ('Witness: name withheld, as promised. Noted.'), and says why if she's asked.\n\n**Statblock:** guard\n\n**Weapon:** club\n\n**Knows**\n\n- Whatever was said at the granary (and, after dark, at the Rat) is in her notebook, as it was said; asked, she reads an entry back word for word, and that's all.\n- In the afternoon: she took a pie round to the granary porters at their noon break (porters will talk to a pie when they won't talk to the watch), and while they ate, the Purveyor's flour wagons went by with a gentleman walking beside them (a silver-topped cane, a wheat-ear ring, lavender water), and at his elbow a big dock-man nursing a bandaged right hand, who called him 'Master' and stopped himself. She knows neither of them by name, nor the gentleman by sight. Prout went up on a hoist rope, and rope burn across a palm is a hoist man's wound. When the wagons went up the hill she followed the big man down the river stairs and saw him go into the Drowned Rat, halfway down; she didn't go in after him.\n- If Prout went into the Captain's book as a murder this morning, a runner has brought word that the Captain will have her report 'after the Assembly'. She means to have the case made before he reads it.\n- The palace is shut to anyone without business until the Assembly, but the gate guard will take a written message for the Marshal, and it reaches him by evening.\n- After dark: her whistle brings nobody but her. The whole watch is lining the Assembly route through the night.\n\n**Secrets**\n\n- Prout's visit three days ago, and her sending him to the palace. If it has come out, it's told, and she doesn't tell it again. If not, it may come out now, once, in her own words, if the investigator asks her straight why this one matters.\n\n## Limits\n\n- Going anywhere else tonight (the palace, home, the watch house): the barge sails on the ebb after midnight, and whatever is in the Heron goes with it.\n- More help: the whole watch is lining the Assembly route through the night, the river watch with it, and Kell's whistle brings nobody but Kell.\n\n## Essentials\n\n### lead\n\nThe Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves, is loading a barge tonight that sails on the ebb at the second bell after midnight; if Hobb talked, the gentleman comes at midnight to see it off.\n\n**Placed with:** Sergeant Kell's own errand (she's going there); gossip in the town\n\n**Unlocks:** the next scene\n\n**Carriers:** kell\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The player hasn't yet gone into the Heron warehouse or dealt with what happens there tonight (the loading, the true book, the Master's visit).\n\n## Scene context\n\n- Time: night\n- Overtaken continuation: heron-warehouse\n- Downtime interlude: yes\n- Fronts document: World/Fronts.md\n\n## Import note\n\nEditable local authoring copy of the main game scene. Script functions and their supporting source files are retained as inert text, never executed by this import or editor. The source snapshot does not change when graph content is edited.",
